"Learn more about the books in the Khan Academy Kids app – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4409036780955-Learn-more-about-the-books-in-the-Khan-Academy-Kids-app)\nciteturn961view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4409036780955-Learn-more-about-the-books-in-the-Khan-Academy-Kids-app\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 122\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Five ways to use Khan Kids for back to school success】\nL9:   * 【4†Quick start guide for preschool through 2nd grade teachers】\nL10:   * 【5†✨ NEW: Teacher Dashboard on the web】\nL11:   * 【6†✨ NEW: Manage student access to areas of the app】\nL12:   * 【7†Module 1: Setting up a Class Account】\nL13:   * 【8†Learning Topics: Using Khan Academy Kids in Educational Settings】\nL14:   * 【9†Module 2: Using Class Codes】\nL15:   * 【10†Daily Schedules for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†Module 3: Assigning lessons】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Learn more about the books in the Khan Academy Kids app\nL19: \nL20:   * February 26, 2026 23:43\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: The Khan Academy Kids app has over 300 books for kids to enjoy and is constantly growing! Our wide variety of fiction texts explore social-emotional topics with cute characters and engaging story lines. There's a whole world of non-fiction texts too, that will tell learners all about animals, space, trucks, insects, and so much more. Click 【13†here】 to learn more about the different types of books that you can find in Khan Academy Kids.\nL23:   * 【14†How to find books in Khan Kids】\nL24:   * 【15†Books with embedded questions】\nL25:   * 【13†Assigning books to your students】\nL26:   * 【13†Overview of different types of books】\nL27:   * 【16†Printable activity packets】\nL28: ### How to find books in Khan Kids\nL29: \nL30: Students can open Khan Academy Kids to the Home Screen (the page with the big house), and tap the purple book icon that says “Library” in the upper left corner. This will take them to the Library, where they can browse through our full offering of books.\nL31: \nL32: 【17†Image: studentlibrary.png】\nL33: If you have a 【18†Class Account】, you can access these books from your teacher view. Across the top of the screen, you will see tabs for Letters, Reading, Math, Logic+, Books and Videos. Go to the Books tab and scroll down to view the different sections.\nL34: All the books in the Books tab can be found in the Reading tab as well. Go to the Reading tab and change the gray dropdown menu on the upper right side of the tab. Scroll down until you see a section with books. There are lots of sections, so you might be scrolling for a little while!\nL35: ### Books with embedded questions\nL36: \nL37: All of our books can be found in both the Books tab and the Reading tab.\nL38: \nL39: 【19†Image: bookstab.png】  | 【20†Image: readingtab.png】\nL40: \nL41: When a book is selected from the Books tab, there are two options: Read to Me or Read by Myself. If learners select Read to Me, the story will be read aloud and the text will highlight at the same time. If learners select Read by Myself, there will not be any audio and learners will use the arrows to turn the pages.\nL42: When a book is selected from the Reading tab, it includes interactive elements that make turn the book reading experience into a full lesson. Books in the Reading tab will include questions about the text, prompts to tap images on the screen, and sometimes a book walk at the beginning. Kodi will ask comprehension questions throughout, and she'll ask critical thinking questions too.\nL43: There are often embedded lessons that use the pages of the book to teach things like pronouns, sight words, letter sounds, and more. As kids reach 1st and 2nd grade learning levels, the texts will also include some multiple choice questions.\nL44: ### Assigning books to your students\nL45: \nL46: If you have a 【18†Class Account】, you can assign books to your students! You can do this in the same way that you'd 【21†assign any lesson】 in the Khan Kids app. Browse through the Books or Reading tab and tap any book to assign! Or, 【22†assign from the Class Report】  in the sections titled Reading Literature and Reading Informational Text. This is a great opportunity to assign a book in the Khan Kids app that is related to a topic you're discussing in class.\nL47: ### Overview of different types of books\nL48: \nL49: Click on one of the following types of books to read a short description:\nL50: \nL51: 【23†Non-fiction】  | 【24†Stories with Lessons】  | 【25†Early Readers】\nL52: 【26†National Geographic Young Explorer】  | 【27†Alphabet Books】  |\nL53: \nL54: ####\nL55: #### Non-fiction\nL56: \nL57: Our non-fiction texts are created in collaboration with Bellwether Media. Topics include animal groups like cats and dogs, a series on dinosaurs, an exploration of space, and more!\nL58: \nL59: 【28†Image: bellwether.png】  | 【29†Image: simulator_screenshot_B2F2FA53-698D-45FC-8D23-B13B3E631254.png】\nL60: \nL61: ####\nL62: \nL63: #### Stories with Lessons\nL64: #### The adorable characters in these stories make social-emotional topics easy to understand and fun to engage with. T-rex learns it's okay to be himself, even if he likes different things than other carnivores do. Crocodile learns how to cope and be brave without his mom there. Flamingo goes to school for the first time and overcomes his nerves. These relatable emotions and bright illustrations make stories that kids want to read again and again.\nL65: 【30†Image: storieswithlessons.png】  | 【31†Image: gottobeme.png】\nL66: #### Early Readers\nL67: \nL68: The Khan Kids characters are featured front and center in all of our Early Readers. These books are written so that emergent readers can practice their new skill, and 1st and 2nd graders can start reading at a more advanced level. All along the way, Kodi & friends have tons of adventures and learn some valuable lessons! Look for the Early Readers in the Reading tab to see what lessons are embedded—you'll find sight words, letter sounds, blends, and more.\nL69: 【20†Image: readingtab.png】  | 【32†Image: lookatmymap.png】\nL70: ####\nL71: \nL72: #### National Geographic Young Explorer\nL73: \nL74: Kids can start learning about science and research topics with our National Geographic Young Explorer series. This series takes kids around the world, taking a close look at animals, environmental issues, and different climates.\nL75: \nL76: 【33†Image: natgeo.png】  | 【34†Image: plants.png】\nL77: \nL78: ####\nL79: #### Alphabet Books\nL80: \nL81: Scroll down to the very bottom of the Books tab to find a book on each letter in the alphabet! Each book uses words with the letter, and many of them have pictures that kids can move. Can you look in the leaves? Move the leaves to the side to see what's underneath!\nL82: \nL83: 【35†Image: alphabetbooks.png】  | 【36†Image: lookinleaves.png】\nL84: ### Printable activity packets\nL85: \nL86: After students read or listen to a book, it's helpful for them to engage in an activity related to what they just read. \"Extension activities\" can help students remember important plot points, make connections with their own lived experience, and internalize the story's lesson.\nL87: The Khan Academy Kids team has put together three printable packets to accompany books in the Khan Kids app. Each packet includes a coloring page and a writing activity. Click 【37†here】 to download packets for the books Breakfast Time, In Harmony, and Sidewalk Chalk.\nL88: \nL89: Remember, even students who can't read or write full words can engage in writing prompts! They can draw simple pictures and narrate words for an adult to write on their behalf.\nL90: ### Download printable activity packets\nL91: \nL92:   * 【38†KhanKids_BreakfastTime_WritingAndColoring.pdf】\nL93: \nL94: 500 KB 【38†Download】\nL95: \nL96:   * 【39†KhanKids_InHarmony_WritingAndColoring.pdf】\nL97: \nL98: 800 KB 【39†Download】\nL99: \nL100:   * 【40†KhanKids_SidewalkChalk_WritingAndColoring.pdf】\nL101: \nL102: 400 KB 【40†Download】\nL103: \nL104: Was this article helpful?\nL105: \nL106: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL107: \nL108: 3 out of 3 found this helpful\nL109: \nL110: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL111: \nL112: 【41†Return to top】\nL113: ## Comments\nL114: \nL115: 0 comments\nL116: \nL117: Article is closed for comments.\nL118: \nL119: 【42†Khan Academy】\nL120: \nL121: 【43†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy can't I hear the sound? – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/360007053151-Why-can-t-I-hear-the-sound)\nciteturn961view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn959view0\",\"id\":5}); Total lines: 63\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Get in touch!】\nL9:   * 【4†Welcome to Khan Academy Kids】\nL10:   * 【5†Parent and Home Account Users: Getting Started and creating an account with Khan Academy Kids】\nL11:   * 【6†How do I set up an account?】\nL12:   * 【7†How do I verify my email?】\nL13:   * 【8†List of resources for parents】\nL14:   * 【9†Printable activities for parents and teachers】\nL15:   * 【10†Weekly Planners for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†How does the learning level adjust and how do I view my child's progress?】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Why can't I hear the sound?\nL19: \nL20:   * February 27, 2026 00:11\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: ## If you cannot hear sound in the app, first check the following on your device:\nL23: \nL24: 1. Mute: Make sure the device is not muted. Sometimes videos or music in other apps still play, even on mute, so the mute settings are important to double check.\nL25: \nL26:   * For iPhone, make sure the orange dot is not showing on the side of the device.\nL27:   * For iPad, go to 【13†Control Center†support.apple.com】 and make sure the bell icon is not selected. If the bell icon is crossed out, this will mute the app.\nL28: 【14†Image: iphone6-ringer-silent.png】【15†Image: iPad_Settings.jpg】\nL29: \nL30: 2. Side switch settings / Lock rotation: If you are using an iPad with a side switch, and do not get sound even after moving the side switch, check the setting for the side switch (on older versions of iOS, go to Settings > General > Lock Rotation / Mute) and ensure that lock rotation is not selected for for the side switch.\nL31: \nL32: 3. Volume: Check the volume buttons to make sure the sound is turned up when you are in the app.\nL33: 4. Bluetooth: Try turning bluetooth off to make sure sound is not being routed to external audio output. To test, go to Settings > Bluetooth and toggle to off. Or for iPad, go to Control Center and make sure the bluetooth icon (shown above) is not selected.\nL34: ## If the steps above do not bring back sound on the app, please proceed with the following:\nL35: \nL36: 4. Verify device volume: Verify that the volume works by running a sound test. For iOS devices, go to Settings > Sounds and move the Ringer and Alerts slider up and down. If you do hear sound, follow 【16†these steps to resolve sound on Apple devices†support.apple.com】.\nL37: \nL38: 5. Volume limit: For iOS devices, go to Settings > Music > Volume Limit to check if a volume limit is set for the device.\nL39: 6. Headphones: If you can hear sound through headphones but not through the device's speakers, try clearing the debris in the headphone jack or a 【17†few tips from this video†www.youtube.com】.\nL40: \nL41: 7. Control Center: Check audio output in the iOS Control Center by swiping up on the home screen. Ensure that audio output for music is not headed outbound to bluetooth, airplay or external speakers. More information about managing audio from the control center is provided 【13†here†support.apple.com】.\nL42: 8. Mute audio test: From your device, open your browser and link to this 【18†Google Doodle sound game†www.google.com】. If you tap and play the game and do not hear sound, your device is muted. Please follow the steps in the first section to unmute the device.\nL43: \nL44: 9. Reboot: Power off and 【19†restart†support.apple.com】 the device, or try resetting to factory defaults.\nL45: \nL46: Was this article helpful?\nL47: \nL48: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL49: \nL50: 6 out of 10 found this helpful\nL51: \nL52: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL53: 【20†Return to top】\nL54: ## Comments\nL55: \nL56: 0 comments\nL57: \nL58: Article is closed for comments.\nL59: \nL60: 【21†Khan Academy】\nL61: \nL62: 【22†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow do I delete an app and re-download? – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/360006761532-How-do-I-delete-an-app-and-re-download)\nciteturn961view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn959view0\",\"id\":2}); Total lines: 55\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Get in touch!】\nL9:   * 【4†Welcome to Khan Academy Kids】\nL10:   * 【5†Parent and Home Account Users: Getting Started and creating an account with Khan Academy Kids】\nL11:   * 【6†How do I set up an account?】\nL12:   * 【7†How do I verify my email?】\nL13:   * 【8†List of resources for parents】\nL14:   * 【9†Printable activities for parents and teachers】\nL15:   * 【10†Weekly Planners for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†How does the learning level adjust and how do I view my child's progress?】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # How do I delete an app and re-download?\nL19: \nL20:   * February 27, 2026 00:11\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: ## Apple iOS devices\nL23:   * To delete the application from your device, press down on the app icon you want to delete until all of the icons start shaking\nL24:   * An X should appear on the top corner of the icons\nL25:   * Press the X of the app icon you want to delete\nL26:   * Open the App Store, search for the app, then re-download the application with the same iTunes login\nL27:   * If you are re-downloading Khan Academy Kids, no payment is necessary since Khan Academy Kids is completely free to download\nL28:   * Please restart your device after re-downloading (see \"【13†How to I restart my device】\")\nL29: ## Android devices\nL30: \nL31:   * Open Settings on your device\nL32:   * Tap on Apps or Application manager\nL33:   * Tap on the app you want to remove (you may need to scroll to find the right one)\nL34:   * Tap Uninstall\nL35: \nL36: Was this article helpful?\nL37: \nL38: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL39: \nL40: 9 out of 10 found this helpful\nL41: \nL42: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL43: \nL44: 【14†Return to top】\nL45: \nL46: ## Comments\nL47: \nL48: 0 comments\nL49: \nL50: Article is closed for comments.\nL51: \nL52: 【15†Khan Academy】\nL53: \nL54: 【16†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProgress reports in the Khan Academy Kids app – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4403614100109-Progress-reports-in-the-Khan-Academy-Kids-app)\nciteturn961view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn959view1\",\"id\":2}); Total lines: 93\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Five ways to use Khan Kids for back to school success】\nL9:   * 【4†Quick start guide for preschool through 2nd grade teachers】\nL10:   * 【5†✨ NEW: Teacher Dashboard on the web】\nL11:   * 【6†✨ NEW: Manage student access to areas of the app】\nL12:   * 【7†Module 1: Setting up a Class Account】\nL13:   * 【8†Learning Topics: Using Khan Academy Kids in Educational Settings】\nL14:   * 【9†Module 2: Using Class Codes】\nL15:   * 【10†Daily Schedules for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†Module 3: Assigning lessons】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Progress reports in the Khan Academy Kids app\nL19: \nL20:   * February 23, 2026 23:05\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: \nL23: 【13†Image: Reports_Banner.png】\nL24: The Khan Academy Kids app has progress reports that allow teachers to identify student needs at a glance. Use the Assignments Report to see which students have completed their assignments, or use the All Progress Report to see cumulative student progress in each subject area. The best part? You can respond to student data in real time by assigning lessons directly from the reports. Read below to learn more!\nL25: ## What can you do with the progress reports in Khan Academy Kids?\nL26: \nL27:   * Assign, edit, and preview lessons directly from the report\nL28:   * See students’ score history on each lesson\nL29:   * Quickly see how far students have progressed to each domain\nL30:   * Identify students who have not completed assignments\nL31:   * Email reports to your desktop computer for easy viewing\nL32:   * Email individual student reports to parents\nL33: \nL34: Here’s what the progress reports look like! Need a close up? Look 【14†here】.\nL35: 【15†Image: ClassReport_AllProgress__1_.png】  | 【16†Image: ClassReport_Assignments.png】\nL36: \nL37: Access these reports from your teacher view in the Khan Academy Kids app. Make sure you are on the Students tab, and then select Class Reports.\nL38: \nL39: 【17†Image: StudentsTab_ClassReports.png】\nL40: \nL41: There are two different reports. You can switch between the two by tapping Assignments or All Progress at the top of the screen.\nL42: \nL43: Use the Assignments report if...  | Use the All Progress report if...\nL44:   * You’d like to see which students have assignments that haven’t been completed yet.\nL45:   * You’d like to check to see if students attempted an assignment multiple times.\nL46:   * You’d like to edit an existing assignment, either by changing the date students receive it, or changing which students receive it.\nL47:   * Learn more about the Assignments report 【14†here】.\nL48:  |\nL49:   * You’d like to see students’ overall progress in a given subject area (like ELA), or in a given domain (like Counting & Cardinality).\nL50:   * You’d like to make instructional decisions based on student scores. If you see that a student received a low score on a Main assignment, you can assign the Basic lesson to provide them with additional scaffolding. Tap Basic directly on this screen to assign.\nL51:   * Learn more about the All Progress report 【18†here】.\nL52: ### The Assignments report\nL53: \nL54:   * The Assignments report shows all of the lessons you’ve assigned and all student scores.\nL55:   * It’s easy to see which students have completed assignments.\nL56:   * If students have made multiple attempts on a lesson, you can see their score history. Tap on one of the checkmarks to view all attempts.\nL57:   * Tap the lesson thumbnails on the left to edit the assignment or to view the lesson.\nL58: \nL59: 【19†Image: 5-25-21_Report_Dialog_Assignments_iPad.png】\nL60: ### The All Progress report\nL61:   * The All Progress report shows every lesson that students have completed, whether it was sent to them as an assignment, an activity they chose from the Library, or lessons from their Learning Path.\nL62:   * It’s easy to see overall progress in a subject area. The fractions show how many skills have been mastered.\nL63:   * If students have made multiple attempts on a lesson, you can see their score history. Tap on one of the percentages to view all attempts.\nL64:   * Tap the lesson titles on the left to edit the assignment or to view the lesson.\nL65:   * Respond to student scores immediately. If you see that a student received a low score on a Main assignment, then assign the Basic lesson to provide them with additional scaffolding. Tap Basic directly on this screen to assign.\nL66: 【20†Image: Progress_article.png】\nL67: #### Email reports\nL68: \nL69: You can email these reports to yourself so you can view them on a computer. Or, you can use filters to select an individual student and send their report to their parent or guardian. Tap the Email button in the top right corner. Enter an email address in the \"To\" field to send.\nL70: \nL71: 【21†Image: ClassReport_Arrow_Email.png】  | 【22†Image: EmailDialogue.png】\nL72: When you or a student's family member receives the email, there will be an attachment in the form of a ZIP file. Unzip the file to see an HTML file that will open up inside of a web browser.\nL73: \nL74: Please note that your mobile device must be connected to an email address in the device's Settings in order for this to work. That way the app has a \"From\" address to reference when it sends the email.\nL75: \nL76: Was this article helpful?\nL77: \nL78: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL79: \nL80: 1 out of 1 found this helpful\nL81: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL82: \nL83: 【23†Return to top】\nL84: ## Comments\nL85: \nL86: 0 comments\nL87: \nL88: Article is closed for comments.\nL89: \nL90: 【24†Khan Academy】\nL91: \nL92: 【25†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFluency: The key to independent reading – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4403706778637-Fluency-The-key-to-independent-reading)\nciteturn961view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn958view0\",\"id\":16}); Total lines: 76\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Get in touch!】\nL9:   * 【4†Welcome to Khan Academy Kids】\nL10:   * 【5†Parent and Home Account Users: Getting Started and creating an account with Khan Academy Kids】\nL11:   * 【6†How do I set up an account?】\nL12:   * 【7†How do I verify my email?】\nL13:   * 【8†List of resources for parents】\nL14:   * 【9†Printable activities for parents and teachers】\nL15:   * 【10†Weekly Planners for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†How does the learning level adjust and how do I view my child's progress?】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Fluency: The key to independent reading\nL19: \nL20:   * February 26, 2026 23:51\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: ## What is fluency?\nL23: \nL24: When a child begins to move into the world of independent reading, there are several skills and strategies we can use to help them become happy, confident readers.\nL25: After a child has the building blocks of reading, the main focus shifts to developing a child's fluency as they begin to read independently. A fluent reader reads smoothly. They are no longer sounding out words or reading word by word. They also read with expression—meaning that they read with emotion and pauses that make it sound natural. Fluent readers also read with accuracy. They say all the words on the page without skipping or replacing any.\nL26: ## How can I help my child develop fluency skills?\nL27: \nL28: A fluent reader will have an easier time understanding what they read and is more likely to enjoy reading. To help a child develop fluency, you can provide practice with sight words and with decodable texts.\nL29:   * Sight words are words that kids should be able to recognize and say instantly. They are very common words and are often tricky to read and spell because they don’t match regular phonics rules. Words like \"the,\" \"and,\" and \"is\" are all sight words.\nL30:   * Decodable texts are stories that are written completely using sight words and words that young learners can sound out using basic phonics skills.\nL31: \nL32: Here are even more ways to help your child develop fluency. You can 【13†download】 this tip sheet below.\nL33: 【14†Image: KhanKids_Fluency.png】\nL34: ## How can the Khan Kids app help develop fluency skills?\nL35: Helping to build fluency is something we love to do at 【15†Khan Academy Kids†learn.khanacademy.org】! Your learner can practice sight words right in our app. In the Library, select the Reading tab and choose 1st or 2nd grade from the dropdown menu. Scroll down until you get to Sight Words. Let your child practice one or two lessons at a time. Wait a few days and then go back to that same lesson to see if they still remember them. It’s normal to take a lot of exposure to memorize new sight words.\nL36: 【16†Image: Coloring_Pages.png】\nL37: We also have decodable texts throughout our library. In the Books tab, scroll down to the Early Readers section of the Library. These stories are written using only sight words and words a young reader should be able to sound out. First, have them listen to the story read aloud by selecting the “Read to Me” button. Next, have them read the same story, this time choosing “Read by Myself.\" Have your child read the book aloud, and repeat the reading until they sound fluent.\nL38: Be sure to stop or go back to listening to the book if they start to feel frustrated. For older children, you can try this same process using the 1st and 2nd Grade Early Readers in the Library. These books are also decodable, but they will have more difficult phonics patterns.\nL39: With over 300 books in the Khan Kids library, there are plenty of stories to choose from to help build your child’s fluency.\nL40: ## Additional articles\nL41: \nL42: This is article #3 in our series of 5 articles about the path to reading. If you’d like to learn more, be sure to read the other articles!\nL43: \nL44: Article 1) 【17†Pre-literacy】: the period of time before a child begins to read or write\nL45: \nL46: Article 2) 【18†Phonics】: the connection between letters and sounds\nL47: \nL48: Article 4) 【19†Comprehension】: understanding and thinking about what you read\nL49: \nL50: Article 5) 【20†Reading routines】: developing lifelong readers\nL51: ## 👇 Download our fluency tip sheet\nL52: \nL53:   * 【21†KhanKids_Fluency.pdf】\nL54: \nL55: 600 KB 【21†Download】\nL56: \nL57: Was this article helpful?\nL58: \nL59: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL60: \nL61: 3 out of 3 found this helpful\nL62: \nL63: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL64: \nL65: 【22†Return to top】\nL66: \nL67: ## Comments\nL68: \nL69: 0 comments\nL70: \nL71: Article is closed for comments.\nL72: \nL73: 【23†Khan Academy】\nL74: \nL75: 【24†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSandy's Instruments and Reya's Bugs – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/360053927172-Sandy-s-Instruments-and-Reya-s-Bugs)\nciteturn961view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn958view4\",\"id\":7}); Total lines: 60\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Festive winter learning with Khan Academy Kids】\nL8:   * 【3†Meet inspiring women in our new books!】\nL9:   * 【4†Meet a new set of friends in the Khan Kids app!】\nL10:   * 【5†Find books and lessons in the Khan Kids Library】\nL11:   * 【6†How do I save artwork from the Gallery?】\nL12:   * 【7†Sandy's Instruments and Reya's Bugs】\nL13:   * 【8†Podcast Recommendations for Kids】\nL14:   * 【9†Learn more about the characters inside Khan Academy Kids】\nL15:   * 【10†Research from University of Massachusetts Amherst shows Khan Academy Kids boosts pre-literacy skills】\nL16:   * 【11†New first-grade lessons in Khan Academy Kids!】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: 